WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:07.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.969
We turn now to Board committee feedback.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:14.910
I am going to invite David to say a few words
about the Commissioning Committee earlier

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:15.910
this week.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:19.740
We do not have a written report, but David
we would welcome some headline news if we

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.740
may.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.740
&gt;&gt; David Roberts: Thank you, yes.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.820
There will be a written report I think at
the next Board.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.529
The Commissioning Committee was held on Tuesday.

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:33.289
As ever, a broad range of areas discussed.

00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:40.170
We started with the management report from
direct commissioning and looked at a range

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.940
of activities, including section 7a and so
on.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:52.550
Perhaps one of the most important areas there
is the proposed transfer to NHS England of

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.421
the commissioning of healthcare within police
custody, which is an area that I do not think

00:00:57.421 --> 00:01:01.899
anyone has been particularly happy with where
we have been.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:08.000
I am pleased to report that there is a clear
set of proposals that have been well bought

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.670
into by all the various parties, and that
the Commissioning Committee agreed that that

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:22.259
transfer should take place, subject to a bit
of a wrangle about some money at the edges

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:27.299
which Paul and crew are in the process of
sorting out.

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:33.459
However, I think it is really important that
that gets done and that we can bring much

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:39.389
needed consistency and improvements to the
healthcare provision to some people who need

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:41.419
it.

00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:54.170
We then looked at the commissioning standards
for out of hours support, including NHS 111

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:01.239
and the integration of 111 with the out-of-hours,
out-of-hospital care.

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:08.060
This is a really good piece of work that has
involved extensive consultation, collaboration

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:16.150
and co-design with various interesting parties
including NHS clinical commissioners and many

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.680
others.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.569
I think the team in Barbara's area have done
a really good job actually in coming up with

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:30.349
something that is essentially the front door
into the urgent and emergency care reviews

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:34.439
and networks that Bruce talked about.

00:02:34.439 --> 00:02:39.150
We are now into the stage of now having got
the commissioning standards approved and ready

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.150
to go.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:44.930
We are now into the real work, which is how
we then start to bring this to fruition, which

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:55.269
is all about collaboration and about working
together locally to produce good strong solutions.

00:02:55.269 --> 00:02:56.530
We have good providers in place.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:57.530
We have a good solution.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:02.049
We have a good set of standards, and I think
this will make a very significant difference

00:03:02.049 --> 00:03:05.230
over time.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:13.519
We then moved to the annual assurance assessment
of CCGs, which reflects the environment we

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:19.730
are in and the challenges faced, but also
reflects I think continued progress across

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:29.640
the CCGs in building capability and in building
their planning capabilities in particular.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:36.400
I would also recognise that the standards
are tougher that we are also putting in place.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.780
Finally, and perhaps in some respects as important
as any of those together, we have a regular

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:51.350
programme of looking at the ongoing reviews
on the various health improvement areas.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:56.849
In this case cancer, mental health and maternity.

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:04.599
Cancer, where the report is in place and we
are in implementation planning, there is new

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:09.700
resource being brought to bear and I think
the committee felt that good progress was

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:10.939
being made.

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:19.000
However, we need to continue to keep real
attention on how we take the 90 plus recommendations

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.750
to prioritise those, and to make sure that
we get on and deliver the most important ones

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:30.650
quickly without losing sight of the overall
set of recommendations.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:37.251
Mental health and maternity was more an update
on where the taskforces had got to, and when

00:04:37.251 --> 00:04:44.530
we were likely to see delivery and indeed
the process of engagement between NHS England

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.960
and those taskforces.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.949
Because we need to make sure that everything
that is produced is both impactful and also

00:04:51.949 --> 00:04:55.110
deliverable, and that work is in good shape.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:00.810
We will come back to those in due course future
committee meetings.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:09.740
My overall sense is significant amounts of
work ongoing broadly in the right areas, and

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:14.430
with good traction and progress, and I am
really pleased to see really good collaboration

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.419
with the CCGs and the various elements of
the health system.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:23.330
Where that collaboration is in place we get
faster and better delivery; where it is not,

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:24.330
we do not.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.190
I think that is an important lesson for all
of us.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:28.319
Happy to take any questions.

00:05:28.319 --> 00:05:32.009
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes, any questions, comments
for David?

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:35.240
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Just one word of clarification.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.190
You referred to wrangles around the funding
of police custody.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.070
There are not any wrangles at the moment.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:45.160
I think what you might be referring to is
my natural finance director's vigilance that

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.520
the agreements that have been breached around
this particular thing get sustained all the

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.500
way through to the money arriving as the service
gets transferred to us, but I would not like

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.060
to give the Board the impression there are
outstanding matters of that sort which we

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.300
are currently struggling with.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.410
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: That is entirely appropriate,
putting me right.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:01.410
Thank you.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:08.740
My only rejoinder to that would be - and this
is not really aimed at yourself, it is aimed

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:15.131
at the broader audience - that if that work
is to come across and we are to make the improvements,

00:06:15.131 --> 00:06:19.479
the finances at the appropriate level have
to cover that.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.290
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Indeed.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:26.300
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We have two other committee
reports which are amongst the papers that

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.300
you have got.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.840
One on investment committee.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.330
Moira, may we take the paper as read?

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:36.340
May I invite any comments, questions on investment
committee?

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:37.340
No.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.340
Thank you.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.449
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Specialised services; Noel,
any comment you would wish to make?

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:47.039
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I had not planned to say very
much at all Malcolm about this, so I am happy

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:50.700
to take it as read.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.930
Our work plan is established more or less
for the rest of the year.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:58.789
As you would have noted from the last time
I spoke at the July Board meeting, we issued

00:06:58.789 --> 00:07:04.639
our commissioning intentions for the year
at the end of June to do with our rare drug

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:06.280
approvals and treatments.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:12.740
I only mention one thing that is not fully
captured in the report to the Board, and that

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:18.720
is that we are revising our approach to public
engagement on specialised services.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:26.620
It had set a trend when we went through the
consultation process and the feedback from

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.620
the consultation process.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:36.270
We have learned a lot from that, and the public
engagement process itself is on our agenda

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.449
to enhance as we go forward.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:42.740
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:43.740
Moira?

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:51.021
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Perhaps Noel could tell us what
ultra orphan drugs are?

00:07:51.021 --> 00:07:52.021
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Ultra-orphan drugs.

00:07:52.021 --> 00:07:54.889
Well I might defer to my learned colleagues
here.

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:59.740
&gt;&gt;Barbara Hakin: They are for very, very rare
disorders.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:04.319
So orphan are for rare disorders, and ultra-orphan
are for very, very rare disorders.

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:10.199
They are the sorts of treatments that cost
a very great deal to produce but are actually

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:15.770
only used in a very small number of patients,
and therefore need specific ways of dealing

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.969
with how we fund them.

00:08:17.969 --> 00:08:21.840
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you Barbara, for that
lucid explanation.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.189
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think we are done on committee
reports.

00:08:25.189 --> 00:08:26.969
Thank you very much.

00:08:26.969 --> 00:08:32.450
The next item is the Annual General Meeting,
and it falls to me just to say a few words

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:34.130
about this.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:39.150
To have an AGM is a formal expectation of
a Board such as this, and it gives us an opportunity

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:42.861
to present our annual report and accounts.

00:08:42.861 --> 00:08:48.130
Again, thanks to Paul and so many other people
right across the organisation for the efficiency

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.150
and clarity with which those were produced
this year.

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:59.140
We are proposing to have the meeting of the
Board on 21st October.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:02.050
We are going to hold it in the Kia Oval in
Kennington.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:09.420
It will be a public meeting, but in addition
to having the formal presentation of the annual

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:14.250
report and accounts, we want to use this as
an opportunity to engage with a much broader

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:22.010
array of stakeholders, and particularly to
dive much more deeply into what we have been

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.750
doing over the past year in relation to a
five year forward view and to engage with

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.940
vanguards and other actors in that process.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.280
It is open to the public, and we would like
to encourage as many people as possible to

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:40.820
come and join with us in a discussion about
what we have been doing, where we are going,

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.970
who are partners are and how we are working
collaboratively across the piece.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:48.140
That is all I think I need to say about that.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:52.880
Are there any questions, comments on that?

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:53.950
No.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:58.810
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We move on to any other business.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.570
The Chief Executive has an item and I have
a couple of items.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:07.750
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: It was just to update the
Board on a few things that will land between

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:11.720
now and our next public meeting, which is
20th November.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:17.220
Picking up where Jane left off, she and colleagues
will be publishing the learning disability

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:23.800
closure and transformation plan during October,
so that will have landed by the time we are

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.130
together on 20th November.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:30.280
It is likely that the Mental Health Taskforce
chaired by Paul Farmer will have produced

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.780
its work by then.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:36.250
Talking to Julia Cumberlege and Cyril Chantler
yesterday, I think the maternity task force

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:43.580
is more likely to be the end of December rather
than prior to that, but the substance of where

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.460
they have got to seems to be making great
progress.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:52.790
The work that Bruce has been doing on seven
day services and thinking about the feasible

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:59.050
phasing of the clinical standards that will
tackle the excess mortality in hospitals and

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:03.610
weekends, I think the expectation is that
we should be in a position to make announcements

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:07.480
on that within the next couple of weeks.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:13.050
On the temporary staffing issue we have already
talked about, we expect NHS-wide action on

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:16.250
that very imminently.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:23.350
Winter resilience planning processes are in
hand, and in the second half of October we

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:29.590
expect to nail down what the line of sight
is to what the SRGs are saying on that.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:34.530
Then finally, as we have previously discussed,
there are a number of key appointments that

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:41.350
we will be making to the organisation over
the next six weeks or so, picking up where

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:48.560
Karen left off in her report: a new leader
on primary care, specialised commissioning

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:55.150
and on cancer services, and also the recruitment
process now running in respect of the vacancies

00:11:55.150 --> 00:12:01.860
from Barbara and Tim, which I am working closely
with Ed on in terms of inter-digitating the

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:04.160
division of labour between our two organisations.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.530
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:09.480
So if there are no other items of business,
I just wish to record two.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:15.250
First of all, to report formally to the Board
that my term of office as Chairman comes to

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:19.920
an end in five weeks' time, but that I have
been invited by the Secretary of State to

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.200
serve a second term and I have accepted that
invitation.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:30.110
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Secondly, to report to the
Board that this is the last meeting at which

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:36.790
Ed Smith will be with us, who has served for
the last four years both as Chair of our Audit

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:40.380
and Risk Committee and also as our Deputy
Chairman.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:45.810
I would wish the Board to endorse my expression
of appreciation for what he has done, and

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:49.530
I can characterise Ed's contribution I think
under about four heads.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:55.540
One is the expertise that he has brought from
his vast background experience to the business

00:12:55.540 --> 00:13:00.520
of the Board, and particularly to his chairmanship
of audit and risk.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:06.560
Secondly to his sheer professionalism in the
way in which he has discharged his responsibilities

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.360
as a member of this Board.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:14.360
Thirdly, to his engagement right across the
system; not just seeing his contribution as

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:19.810
confined to this Board, but how he engaged
with other parts of the NHS and our broader

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.290
community of stakeholders.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:28.551
Finally, if I may pay tribute to his enormous
generosity and his values, which I think have

00:13:28.551 --> 00:13:34.300
been immensely powerful in supporting our
team of national directors and have also been

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.490
a model to the rest of us as members of the
Board.

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:44.480
Ed, we are not losing you entirely; we have
retained an umbilical cord, as Ed goes on

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.000
to his chairmanship of NHS improvement.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:54.480
I know that all of those values to which I
have just referred will continue to be reflected

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:00.520
in your responsibilities there, very much
to our advantage, as these organisations come

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:06.610
together to work much more closely to overcome
so many of the silos which can obstruct and

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:11.420
pervert the way in which we would wish to
discharge collectively our responsibilities

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.940
in respect of the NHS.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:19.100
May I say personally, how much I look forward
to continuing to work closely with you?

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:22.050
&gt;&gt;Ed Smith: Thank you, that is incredibly
generous.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:28.170
Can I actually first of all say, I think on
behalf of the whole Board, without any doubt,

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:35.240
how delighted we are that you are going to
do another four years as Chairman of NHS England?

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:39.560
I am normally sad when I leave a board at
the end of a term, but I cannot actually say

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:45.540
that I have that same sadness here, because
I know we are going to be together for quite

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:51.230
a long time into the future, locked in arms
as we tackle the challenges and the opportunities

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:52.230
ahead.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:58.860
Whilst I might not be physically around your
public Board meetings, I know I will be with

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.440
you outside those Board meetings Malcolm,
and I very much look forward to that.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.100
It has been an absolute privilege and a distinct
pleasure to be here for the first four years

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:13.760
of NHS England, and let's just go on and do
what we need to do together.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:21.583
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you Ed, for all that
you have done.

00:15:21.583 --> 00:15:24.160
Right, that brings us to the end of the business
this morning.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.570
May I thank all those who have joined us today,
and thank all of my colleagues.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:30.630
I declare the meeting closed, thank you.

